            "review_text": "kvinh2013\nHo Chi Minh City, Vietnam175 contributions\n0\nRice noddle tofu Ms. Husky Voice\nNov 2016 • Friends\nThe full translation ought to be: North Vietnamese Rice Noodle Tofu Ms. Kusky Voice. That husky voice belongs to the lady owner, a tall good looking lady rumored to be a \"Chân dài\", long legs, the Vietnamese definition of a model. In fact she was seated at the front as our party debarked on that rainy day. I took advantage of the situation and snapped a selfie with the society figure as she happily obliged. Our party of 6 climbed a narrow stairway to get to 2nd floor dinning area and was seated at a corner table by the window. This is a typical popular joint only meant for locals or hard core food adventurers; it's cramped, with low tables and low chairs, minimal decor, in fact no decor, just the bare necessities. The place was packed and buzzing with conversations and the din of shouted orders from the staff. We quickly ordered the dish that this place is famous for \"Bún Đậu\" with all the trimming, kumquat tea and grilled \"nem chua\" a cured pork sausage. As we drank the excellent kumquat tea and nibble on the grilled \"nem\", I scanned the room and found that a good number of customers are fashionably dressed, good looking young ladies; that explained the connection between the owner and the crowd that she attracted I thought. The food arrived in weaved bamboo trays, there was a mound of fresh vegetable, sliced boiled pork, rice noodle, \"chả cốm\" North Vietnamese sausage with encrusted grain of fat, fried tofu and of course, the ingredient that defines this dish; \"mắm tôm\" shrimp paste. For the uninitiated, \"mắm tôm\", the purplish concoction looks intimidating, tastes stunning in a growth arresting way and smells just breathtaking in a choking way. Yet, once one gets the hang of it's delicious and an essential ingredient for many North Vietnamese noodle dishes. We thoroughly enjoyed our very authentic meal. The fried tofu has a slightly crunchy crust and silky smooth on the inside. The rest of the menu includes many hard core dishes such as organ meats prepared several ways and bún (rice noodle) prepared with tofu, snails etc... For a thoroughly authentic North Vietnamese dining experience listen to the husky voice of Ms. Khàn.\nRead more\nValue\nService\nFood\nReview collected in partnership with this restaurant \nWritten December 20, 2016",
